From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 23:00:00 +0330
Subject: [PATCH 01/18] feat(editor): text direction, RTL segments in code, text alignment
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

VERSION: 2.0 (2026-10-12)  2.0 = merged set of 11 patches (old numbers below)
Root: build/outline-1.11.0

Text direction (was 0001 v1.1):
- Paragraph and heading nodes get a dir attribute (rtl, ltr, or null for
  the automatic detection), stored in the document JSON.
- Formatting toolbar: Text direction submenu (RTL, LTR, automatic).
- The direction is kept when switching between paragraph and heading, and
  carried to the new block created by Enter.
- Markdown: an explicit direction is written as a leading RLM/LRM and read
  back into the dir attribute on import; tokens without text are skipped.
- Blockquotes whose first paragraph is RTL are laid out right-to-left.

Right-to-left segments in code (was 0002 v1.0):
- Inline code and code blocks are always LTR and isolated (unicode-bidi:
  isolate), so code no longer reorders surrounding RTL text.
- Toolbar inside code: wrap the selection in RLE (U+202B) or LRE (U+202A)
  ... PDF (U+202C); applying again removes or switches it. With a bare
  cursor in a code block an empty RTL embedding is inserted.
- Bidi control characters inside code are labelled (RLE, PDF, RLI, ...)
  while editing; their effect on the layout is kept.

Text alignment (was 0003 v1.0):
- Paragraph and heading nodes get an align attribute (left, center, right,
  justify, or null for the default), independent of the text direction.
- Formatting toolbar: Text alignment submenu.
- Alignment and direction are kept when switching block types and carried
  to the new block created by Enter (shared helper carryBlockAttr).

